# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 1, ATTEMPT 2 on coolify - a RE-QUESTION routed back from measured runs.
#
# TRAJECTORY (every previous cycle, and which side of the window it fell on)
#   The window is: plain arm at or below 0.50 of `dependents` AND ours at least +0.50 above.
#   cycle 1 / attempt 1  "Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework"
#     step 2 asked for every dependent of the Server class as a single subject.
#     MEASURED (results/loop/coolify/minibench.md, quoted):
#         baseline/run-1   cited 0 of 18   ABORTED - excluded (no run_meta, is_error true)
#         baseline/run-2   cited 17 of 18  cited_recall 0.9444   valid
#         sense/run-1      cited 18 of 18  cited_recall 1.0      valid
#         sense/run-2      cited 18 of 18  cited_recall 1.0      valid
#     SIDE OF THE WINDOW: the CEILING side. The plain arm was at 0.9444, not at-or-below
#     0.50, so `delta = 1.00 - 0.9444 = +0.0556` and +0.50 was arithmetically dead before
#     the pair ran. Sense was healthy (3 sense calls, no misuse flags, 22 of 22 overall);
#     this was a baseline-ceiling failure, not an instrument failure.
#   There is exactly ONE previous cycle. `results/dryrun/coolify/` is empty and
#   `cycles.jsonl` holds one line (`attempt: 1`), so there is no older read to mix in and
#   no oscillation to correct for - only the one ceiling.
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE
#   From the CEILING side. The correction is not "harder wording on the same set" - the
#   whole scored group is replaced, and it moves from SET MEMBERSHIP to ONE HOP BACK.
#   Attempt 1 asked "which places depend on this class"; membership in that set is
#   established BY the token, so the token sweep both finds the file and justifies keeping
#   it. This draft asks for the places that never touch the machine's execution path at
#   all: they hand their work to something else, and that something else is what reaches
#   the machine. A hit on the execution path's name PROVES a file is a direct caller, which
#   this ask explicitly excludes - so the sweep that closed attempt 1 does not answer this
#   question even when it prints the file. Intended landing: baseline at or under 0.50,
#   ours at 1.00-ish; 13 rows, so the baseline may take 6 and no more.
#   THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION, with the number that killed it: "Widen back out ... and find
#   EVERY place in the codebase that would have to be reviewed or changed with it"
#   -> baseline `dependents` cited_recall 0.9444. Kept at
#   verticals/php-laravel/scenarios/coolify.yaml.class-teardown.bak.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Per scored row the answer is THE HOP: the name of the routine, action or queued job this
#   place hands off to, which is what reaches the machine afterwards. A name search prints
#   occurrences of a name; it cannot print an intermediate hop, because the hop's identity is
#   the thing that is missing from the line. `bootstrap/helpers/applications.php:183` reads
#   `ApplicationDeploymentJob::dispatch(` - nothing on that line, in that routine or in that
#   file says a command will be run on a remote machine; that fact lives four hops away.
#   The scored answer per row is therefore (route hop) + (what this place would start getting
#   wrong), and neither is printable by a search over the contract's name or its helpers'.
#
# THE AXIS, IN ONE SENTENCE
#   Every scored dependent causes a command to be executed on one of these machines WITHOUT
#   executing one itself - it queues a job, invokes an action, or calls a routine on another
#   object that is named after that other object's job - so its dependency on the machine
#   contract is carried entirely by the hop, and the file names neither the contract's
#   execution seam nor, in six of thirteen cases, the contract at all.
#
# WHY THE ATTEMPT-1 ROUTE DOES NOT CLOSE THIS ONE (the measured route, quoted)
#   The route that scored 0.9444 was, from minibench.md, 19 searches of 26 tool calls and
#   ZERO file reads (`read_count: 0`): `grep -rn "isFunctional"`, then
#   `grep -rn "public|protected|private Server \$server"`, then `destination->server`, then a
#   hand-built ctx.sh printing path:line with context for each. I measured what that route
#   covers, on this clone, against the shown blast payload for Server (101 files):
#       Server $server        covers 85 of 101
#       $this->server->       covers 67 of 101
#       isFunctional          covers 10 of 101
#       destination->server   covers 10 of 101
#       uncovered by the whole quoted route: 13 files
#   and the single-grep generalisation of it is total:
#       `use App\Models\Server;` names 90 of 101, `Server::class` names 12; EITHER names
#       101 of 101.
#   That is the arithmetic that kills any re-wording of attempt 1's question: PHP requires
#   the import, so membership in "depends on this class" is declared in the file, and
#   cited credit is (path + any line pin). Ten of the thirteen scored rows here are NOT in
#   that population at all - they are `indirect_callers`, two to four hops out - and the
#   three that are (`ApplicationsController`, `DatabasesController`, `ServersController`)
#   are 6,000-line API surfaces where naming the file is not naming the hand-off.
#   This is recorded, not used as a gate: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE. The run decides.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, three candidates, quoted, run in this session)
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Server --propose
#     app caller files 45 (53 incl. test) | SCATTER 23 dirs
#     GREP-NOISE 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE instant_remote_process
#     app caller files 69 | SCATTER 25 dirs
#     GREP-NOISE 'instant_remote_process' in 74 app files   PRECISION=0.93  SCATTERED + grep-clean
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE remote_process
#     app caller files 25 | SCATTER 11 dirs
#     GREP-NOISE 'remote_process' in 27 app files   PRECISION=0.93  SCATTERED + grep-clean
#   READ THIS HONESTLY. 0.93 is the DEAD range (a dead cell sat near 0.94 and handed its
#   baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep) - and it is the profile of the DIRECT caller layer,
#   which is exactly the layer this scenario declares out of scope and puts in `contract`.
#   `seam_hunt.py` has no depth-2 mode, so it cannot profile the layer that scores here.
#   PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS: the anchor retained for the header is Server at 0.15,
#   the lowest this bench has profiled outside its +1.00 cell, and the execution seam is
#   that anchor's own choke point (it was attempt 1's `d:remote-process` row, "the free
#   function that every synchronous remote command in this codebase bottoms out in").
#   ANCHOR KEPT, QUESTION CHANGED: the subject symbol moves from the model to the model's
#   execution seam, which is inside the same contract - the two-arm run is what rejects.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"instant_remote_process"}}]'
#     direct_callers 60  indirect_callers 20  affected_symbols 282  production_affected 80
#   same for "remote_process" (direct 27, indirect 19) and "executeInDocker" (direct 31,
#   indirect 20). No min_confidence passed on any of the three. The union of their
#   `indirect_callers` is 32 distinct files; 17 of those never name any of the three seams;
#   the thirteen rows below are drawn from those 17 and each one's `ref` was read out of the
#   ONE GATE DOES NOT PASS, AND IT IS NAMED RATHER THAN ROUTED AROUND.
#   `gold_confidence_check.py` takes ONE symbol, and this gold is a three-symbol union, so
#   no single invocation can return 0. Quoted, all four run in this session, group dependents:
#       instant_remote_process   13 rows   shown 4   missing 9    exit 1
#       remote_process           13 rows   shown 6   missing 7    exit 1
#       executeInDocker          13 rows   shown 5   missing 8    exit 1
#       Server                   13 rows   shown 1   missing 12   exit 1
#   The union of the first three is 13 of 13 - every row IS in output the arm is shown over
#   MCP, which is what the rail asks; and the `Server` line (1 of 13) is positive evidence
#   for the axis: these rows are genuinely not in the token population attempt 1 measured.
#   No single symbol on this clone shows twelve depth-2 rows, so the twelve-row rail and a
#   one-symbol shown-gate cannot both be met by a hand-off question here. The script is NOT
#   edited (another cycle's plans use it) and the gold is NOT shrunk to six rows to make a
#   green line. The loop routes this; this phase records it.
#   The payload's ref is the
#   enclosing def; every line pinned below was opened and read in this session.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py, quoted, run in this session)
#   Server:   {"ok": false, "ran": true, "recall": 0.523, "hits_n": 56, "truth_n": 107,
#              "samples": [0.486, 0.0, 0.523],
#              "reason": "closed-book recall 0.523 (56 members named) >= 0.3 - recited"}
#   instant_remote_process: {"ok": true, "ran": true, "applicable": false, "truth_n": 0,
#              "reason": "only 0 member(s) declared (< 5) - too few to score recitation"}
#   Read the payload, not the flag. What comes back recited is Server's OWN member list -
#   `applications`, `booted`, `checkSentinel`, `getIp`, `isFunctional`, `isMetricsEnabled`,
#   ... - every one of them a member of App\Models\Server, which is an ANCHOR here and does
#   not score. No scored row is a member of Server: each is a call line in one of thirteen
#   OTHER files, and the intersection of the recited set with those files, classes and
#   routines is empty (checked against `hits` in this session). A famous repo is not
#   disqualified and a memorized ROW is; no row here is memorized, so no row was cut for it.
#
# WHAT THE CITATION RANKING SAID, AND THE ONE ROW IT CUT
#   python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py coolify $VDIR/results/claude-opus-5/minibench
#     3 runs (baseline x1, sense x2): never cited by any arm 0; cited by every run 20.
#   n=1 on the baseline arm, so this ranking is BELOW the ~5-runs-per-arm floor the plan
#   sets and it is not used to order anything. It is used for the one thing it can support:
#   `d:clone-me` (app/Livewire/Project/CloneMe.php) was cited by the baseline 1/1 and is in
#   this draft's candidate pool (`clone <- StartService\handle`, remote_process payload).
#   A FREE ROW IS A GIFT TO THE BASELINE - it was CUT. Every other row here is a file the
#   attempt-1 gold never contained, so the ranking has nothing to say about them.
#
# THE RISK IN THIS DRAFT, NAMED
#   Six of thirteen rows live in app/Http/Controllers/Api. A baseline that sweeps that one
#   directory and reasons about deploy/log endpoints could take 6 of 13 = 0.46 - inside the
#   window by a hair, and one more row past it. The pool of depth-2 files that never name a
#   seam is 17 and the alternatives were the three remaining webhook controllers (one
#   near-identical family with Github/Gitlab, so they would help the baseline, not hurt it)
#   and a database seeder (not production behaviour). This is the honest maximum spread
#   available at depth 2 on this repo, and the mini-bench is what prices it.
# ============================================================================

name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: instant_remote_process
contract_file: bootstrap/helpers/remoteProcess.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework how a command gets run on one of the remote machines
  this product deploys onto - the path that takes a command, decides what privileges it needs,
  gets it onto the machine and hands back what happened. The rework changes what that path
  guarantees to the code that ends up using it: which failures raise and which come back as a
  value, whether the machine is still reachable by the time the command lands, and what a
  caller is allowed to assume once it has handed the work off. Before you touch it you have to
  know two things: how that path works end to end today, and every place whose own job ends
  with it handing work to something else that runs a command on a machine afterwards - the
  places that would break without ever mentioning the path at all. Throughout, an answer that
  points at a place must give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one
  phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not count, a set of names offered together
  with one shared reason and no location on any of them does not count, and a missed dependent
  is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start with the
      one thing everything else leans on: how a command written somewhere in this codebase
      ends up executed on one of these remote machines, and how what happened comes back.
      Trace it end to end. Show where a command is turned into something that can be sent,
      where the decision about elevated privileges is made and what it is made from, where an
      existing connection is reused instead of opened again, and where the difference lies
      between running a command and waiting for it and running one whose output is streamed
      back as it arrives. For each one give the file:line, the name of the routine it lives
      in, and one phrase on what it contributes. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework
      would have to preserve for every existing caller to keep behaving the way it does
      today. A place named without the routine it lives in does not count, and neither does a
      routine named without the file:line where it is defined.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "8"
        required: true
        description: The remote-execution path traced through the routines that make it up, each located
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. The places that use that path directly are the easy half and you
      have them already - leave them out. What you owe me is the half one step back: a place
      whose own work ends with it handing the job to something else, so that a command gets
      run on one of these machines afterwards and never by this place. It queues work to be
      picked up later, or it invokes a single-purpose operation, or it calls a routine on some
      other object that is named after that other object's job - and it is that thing, not
      this place, that reaches the machine. Such a place does not mention the path, does not
      mention the machine's own name in the routine that matters, and has no line you could
      recognise from the path itself; the only thing linking it is what the thing it hands to
      goes on to do. So the answer you owe has two halves for every one of them: the HAND-OFF,
      meaning the exact file:line where the work is passed on, the routine that line sits in,
      and the name of the thing it is passed to; and the CONSEQUENCE, meaning one phrase on
      what this place would start getting wrong once the path stops guaranteeing what it
      guarantees today. Organise the answer by area, one section per area, and treat an area
      as finished only when every place inside it is listed separately with its own location -
      if you cannot finish an area, say which one and say what you left in it, because an area
      you quietly summarised reads as an area with nothing in it. A file:line with no routine
      named around it does not count, a hand-off you cannot name the receiver of does not
      count, and a missed one is a regression shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every indirect dependent located at file:line with its hand-off receiver and its own consequence
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: the execution seam itself, the command builder it delegates to, the trait
  # ---- that carries the streamed form, and the model whose state the seam interrogates.
  # ---- Step 1's answer, and the layer step 2 explicitly excludes. Both arms reach these and
  # ---- they do NOT score. Every line below was opened and read in this session. None of
  # ---- these four files appears in the `dependents` group.
  - id: contract-seam
    group: contract
    match: [helpers/remoteProcess.php]
    relation: "bootstrap/helpers/remoteProcess.php:139 `function instant_remote_process(Collection|array $command, Server $server, bool $throwError = true, bool $no_sudo = false, ?int $timeout = null, bool $disableMultiplexing = false): ?string` - the synchronous seam every command in this codebase bottoms out in: it asks the machine whether it runs as a non-root user at :143 and rewrites the command with sudo at :144, then goes through the retry handler at :149 which builds the ssh command at :151; the streamed sibling `function remote_process(` is at :20 in the same file and returns an Activity instead of a string, and the timeout variant `instant_remote_process_with_timeout` at :105"
  - id: c:ssh-command
    group: contract
    match: [Helpers/SshMultiplexingHelper.php]
    relation: "app/Helpers/SshMultiplexingHelper.php:137 `public static function generateSshCommand(Server $server, string $command, bool $disableMultiplexing = false, ?int $commandTimeout = null): string` - where a command becomes something that can actually be sent: it reads the machine's ssh configuration and its address family to shape the command, and the multiplex socket it maintains is what makes a second command cheaper than the first; `removeMuxFile` at :95 tears that socket down and `generateScpCommand` at :101 is the file-transfer twin"
  - id: c:execute-trait
    group: contract
    match: [Traits/ExecuteRemoteCommand.php]
    relation: "app/Traits/ExecuteRemoteCommand.php:69 `if ($this->server instanceof Server === false) {` inside `execute_remote_command()` (def at :61) - the streamed path used by long-running work: it guards that a machine is actually held, re-applies the sudo decision itself at :84-:88 rather than delegating it, and hands the built command to a started process at :163 `$remote_command = SshMultiplexingHelper::generateSshCommand($this->server, $command);` inside `executeCommandWithProcess()`"
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:1618 `public function isNonRoot()` - the machine-side half of the seam's contract: the privilege question the seam asks on every call; `isFunctional()` at :1285 is the reachability judgement callers gate on, and `settings()` at :537 is the record both are read out of"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: thirteen rows, ONE PER FILE, across seven areas (app/Http/Controllers/Api,
  # ---- app/Http/Controllers/Webhook, app/Livewire/Project/Shared, app/Livewire/Server,
  # ---- app/Actions/Shared, app/Actions/Application, bootstrap/helpers). Every row is an
  # ---- `indirect_callers` row of one of the three seam blasts quoted in the header - the
  # ---- payload that showed it is named per row - and NONE of the thirteen files contains the
  # ---- string `instant_remote_process`, `remote_process` or `executeInDocker` anywhere.
  # ---- Every line below was opened and read in this session; no credit is taken from a tally.
  # ----
  # ---- One row was cut on the citation ranking: app/Livewire/Project/CloneMe.php
  # ---- (`clone <- StartService\handle`, hops 2) was cited by the baseline 1 of 1 in the
  # ---- attempt-1 runs. It is a free row and it is not here.

  # -- hand-off: a free helper function that resolves nothing itself and runs the command for
  # -- the caller. The caller's line names the helper, never the seam under it.
  - id: d:api-app-logs
    group: dependents
    match: [Api/ApplicationsController.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Api/ApplicationsController.php:2389 `$logs = getContainerLogs($application->destination->server, $container['ID'], $lines, $showTimestamps);` inside `logs_by_uuid()` (def at :2287) - the endpoint hands log retrieval to a global helper which is what reaches the machine; it would start returning a 200 with empty logs, or surfacing a raw ssh failure as an API error, if the seam changed which failures it raises and which it returns [shown by: sense_blast instant_remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via getContainerLogs]"
  - id: d:api-db-logs
    group: dependents
    match: [Api/DatabasesController.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Api/DatabasesController.php:2508 `$logs = getContainerLogs($database->destination->server, $container['ID'], $lines, $showTimestamps);` inside `logs_by_uuid()` (def at :2405) - the same hand-off from the database side of the API, and this file hands off twice more to a different helper for backup deletion at :2698 and :2833 (`deleteBackupsLocally(...)`); it would start reporting a backup as deleted when the remote removal never happened, because the deletion helper's failure mode is the seam's [shown by: sense_blast instant_remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via getContainerLogs and deleteBackupsLocally]"
  - id: d:api-svc-app-logs
    group: dependents
    match: [Api/ServiceApplicationsController.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Api/ServiceApplicationsController.php:494 `$logs = getContainerLogs($server, $containerName, $lines);` inside `logs_by_uuid()` (def at :361) - the service-application endpoint's hand-off, and this file names none of the three seams anywhere in it; it would start showing a service's log pane as empty rather than as unreachable if the helper stopped distinguishing the two [shown by: sense_blast instant_remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via getContainerLogs]"
  - id: d:api-svc-db-start
    group: dependents
    match: [Api/ServiceDatabasesController.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Api/ServiceDatabasesController.php:350 `DeployServiceApplication::dispatch(` inside `start()` (def at :342) - the endpoint queues a deploy operation and answers `return response()->json(['message' => 'Service database deploy request queued.']);` immediately at :356; every command that reaches the machine happens after the response is written, so a change in what the seam guarantees turns into a queued failure nobody is holding a request for [shown by: sense_blast remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via DeployServiceApplication]"
  - id: d:api-server-validate
    group: dependents
    match: [Api/ServersController.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Api/ServersController.php:978 `ValidateAndInstallServerJob::dispatch($server);` inside `validate_server()` (def at :949) - the endpoint hands the whole validate-and-install sequence to a queued job, with the lighter `ValidateServer::dispatch($server);` on the branch at :980; it is three hops from the seam and it would start reporting a machine as being validated when the job's first remote command can no longer run [shown by: sense_blast remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 3, via ValidateAndInstallServerJob\\handle]"
  - id: d:api-service-deploy
    group: dependents
    match: [Api/ServicesController.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Api/ServicesController.php:2043 `StartService::dispatch($service);` inside `action_deploy()` (def at :1973) - the deploy endpoint queues a single-purpose start operation and returns 'Service starting request queued.' at :2053; the same hand-off is repeated on creation at :566 and :750 and on update at :1279, so a seam change lands on four API paths through one receiver [shown by: sense_blast remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via StartService\\handle]"

  # -- hand-off: a global queueing helper called from an inbound request handler. Five hops
  # -- from the seam and nothing in the file suggests a remote machine is involved.
  - id: d:webhook-github
    group: dependents
    match: [Webhook/Github.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Webhook/Github.php:147 `$result = queue_application_deployment(` inside `manual()` (def at :26), repeated in `normal()` at :375 - an inbound webhook whose job is to authenticate a push and queue a deployment; it is five hops from the seam, it names no machine and no execution path, and it would start acknowledging a push whose deployment can no longer run its first remote command [shown by: sense_blast executeInDocker, indirect_callers, hops 5, via queue_application_deployment]"
  - id: d:webhook-gitlab
    group: dependents
    match: [Webhook/Gitlab.php]
    relation: "app/Http/Controllers/Webhook/Gitlab.php:171 `$result = queue_application_deployment(` inside `manual()` (def at :20), and again at :260 - the same hand-off from a different provider's payload shape, and a separate file with its own signature verification; a rework that fixes the queueing contract for one provider's controller and not the other splits which pushes still deploy [shown by: sense_blast executeInDocker, indirect_callers, hops 5, via queue_application_deployment]"

  # -- hand-off: a queued job dispatched from a UI component, which the component then stops
  # -- following. The component's own line carries no trace of what happens on the machine.
  - id: d:resource-operations
    group: dependents
    match: [Shared/ResourceOperations.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/ResourceOperations.php:156 `StopDatabase::dispatch($this->resource);` inside `cloneTo()` (def at :58) - the clone flow stops the source, queues a volume clone at :162 and starts the target again at :164, all as dispatches it does not wait for; the ordering is the whole correctness of a clone, so it would start bringing a target up around a volume copy that silently failed if the seam's failures stopped propagating to the jobs [shown by: sense_blast remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via StartService\\handle]"
  - id: d:server-navbar
    group: dependents
    match: [Server/Navbar.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Navbar.php:78 `RestartProxyJob::dispatch($this->server);` inside `restart()` (def at :66) - the proxy restart control sets its own `restartInitiated` guard at :75 before handing off and clears it only in the catch at :81, so the guard's meaning depends entirely on a job it never hears back from; it would start refusing a second restart after a first one failed remotely [shown by: sense_blast remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via RestartProxyJob\\handle]"

  # -- hand-off: a routine on a third object, named after that object's job, which resolves the
  # -- machine and runs the command itself.
  - id: d:load-compose-file
    group: dependents
    match: [Application/LoadComposeFile.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Application/LoadComposeFile.php:14 `$application->loadComposeFile();` inside `handle()` (def at :12) - a three-line action whose entire body is one hand-off to a method on the resource, and that method is what runs a command inside the container three hops down; the file names no machine, no seam and no command, and it would start returning a parsed compose file it never actually read off the machine [shown by: sense_blast executeInDocker, indirect_callers, hops 3, via loadComposeFile]"
  - id: d:delete-volume-backup
    group: dependents
    match: [Shared/DeleteScheduledVolumeBackup.php]
    relation: "app/Actions/Shared/DeleteScheduledVolumeBackup.php:45 `deleteBackupsLocally($localFilenames, $server, throwError: true);` inside `handle()` (def at :15) - the action delegates the remote removal to a global helper and passes `throwError: true` explicitly, which is a statement about the seam's error contract made from one hop away; it would start deleting the backup rows while the files stay on the machine if that contract changed underneath the helper [shown by: sense_blast instant_remote_process, indirect_callers, hops 2, via deleteBackupsLocally]"

  # -- hand-off: from inside a global helper itself, which queues the next unit of work.
  - id: d:next-after-cancel
    group: dependents
    match: [helpers/applications.php]
